`sed` – Displays selected lines from a file.
`head` – Displays the beginning of a file.
`tail` – Displays the ending lines of a file.
`$1` – First command-line argument (start line).
`$2` – Second command-line argument (end line).
`$3` – Third command-line argument (filename).
`-n` – Suppresses automatic printing in sed.
`p` – Prints selected lines.
awk` – Processes and extracts specific lines.
Command-Line Arguments – Values passed to a shell script.
`#!/bin/bash` – Tells the system to execute the script using the Bash shell.
`$#` – Gives the total number of arguments passed to the script.
`-ne` – Checks whether two values are not equal.
`if` – Checks a given condition.
`then` – Executes commands when the condition is true.
`echo` – Displays a message on the screen.
`exit 1` – Terminates the script with an error status.
`"${start},${end}p"` – Specifies the range of lines to be displayed, from the starting line to the ending line.
